Report Writer uses MySQL for templates and task history. Terminology data is
read separately from the configured SPARQL endpoint.

Run these Bash commands from the repository root. The example uses database
reportwriter on local port 3312. Set the credentials in the same shell used
for make run:

    export RW_API_DATASOURCE_URL='jdbc:mysql://127.0.0.1:3312/reportwriter?useSSL=false&allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true'
    export RW_API_DATASOURCE_USERNAME=root
    read -rsp 'MySQL password: ' RW_API_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD
    echo
    export RW_API_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD

Existing database
-----------------
Use its actual credentials and port. If the existing container is named
reportwriter-mysql, start it with:

    docker start reportwriter-mysql

Do not import init.sql into a database you want to keep: it drops and recreates
the tables. Starting an existing container preserves its data.

New local database
------------------
Create a container only if you do not already have a Report Writer database:

    docker run --name reportwriter-mysql \
      -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD="$RW_API_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD" \
      -e MYSQL_DATABASE=reportwriter \
      -p 127.0.0.1:3312:3306 -d mysql:8.4

Wait for MySQL to report ready for connections in docker logs reportwriter-mysql,
then import the schema and templates once:

    docker exec -i -e MYSQL_PWD="$RW_API_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD" \
      reportwriter-mysql mysql -uroot reportwriter < database/init.sql

Verify the database
-------------------
    docker exec -e MYSQL_PWD="$RW_API_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD" \
      reportwriter-mysql mysql -u"$RW_API_DATASOURCE_USERNAME" -D reportwriter \
      -e 'SELECT COUNT(*) AS templates FROM report_template;'

Run make run from the configured shell and open
http://localhost:8080/ncireportwriter2/.
